WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.320
Default credentials, sample accounts, and vendor defaults are common attack targets.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:11.060
Any default identity, token, password, or administrative path should be removed, changed,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.840
or tightly controlled before production use.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:19.340
Open cloud storage and permissive access policies can expose customer files, backups,

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.680
logs, exports, or build artifacts.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.100
Cloud configuration is part of the application security boundary.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.960
Unnecessary services increase attack surface.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.900
Every open port, enabled plugin, feature flag, debug route,

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.880
and administrative function should have a documented purpose and owner.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:44.180
Debug configurations can disclose stack traces, secrets, paths, environment variables,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.160
and internal architecture.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.620
Diagnostic detail belongs in protected logs rather than public responses.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.800
Use secure baselines for applications, systems, containers, and cloud services.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.500
Harden configurations before production release.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.800
Manage configuration changes through reviewed and repeatable processes.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:10.060
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC7.1 - Vulnerability and configuration

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.520
management; CC8.1 - Change control procedures.
